.TH RANDOM 4 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
random, urandom \- kernel random number generators
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/random
.br
.B /dev/urandom
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B random
and
.B urandom
devices provide access to the kernel's random number generator.
.SS /dev/random
This device provides high-quality random bytes derived from hardware entropy
sources. On systems with limited entropy,
.B /dev/random
may block until sufficient entropy is available.
.SS /dev/urandom
This device provides cryptographically secure pseudo-random bytes.
It will never block and is suitable for most cryptographic purposes.
After system initialization,
.B /dev/urandom
provides output indistinguishable from true random data.
.PP
Both devices accept writes, which are mixed into the entropy pool.
.SH NOTES
For most applications, including cryptographic key generation,
.B /dev/urandom
is the recommended source of randomness.
.PP
.B /dev/random
should only be used when true hardware randomness is specifically required.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/random
True random number generator (character device, major 1, minor 8).
.TP
.B /dev/urandom
Cryptographically secure PRNG (character device, major 1, minor 9).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getrandom (2),
.BR intro (4)
